Z||000000||fierogt88||11-01-2000||03:59 PM||||I keep seeing some "fisher body" or "body by fisher" pieces on ebay.  What's the point/significance?||||reg||5
Z||000001||william_thorniii||11-01-2000||04:04 PM||||88,<P>On the early Fiero's (84-86) there was a "Body By Fisher" insert (.5" x 5" approximately) on each of the door sills.  By their frequency on E-bay, I would assume that they either get damaged or are lost from many vehicles.  I have these on both my 85 GT and 86 SE, but they are replaced by a black insert on my 87 EC.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000002||rockcrawl||11-01-2000||04:19 PM||||The long center piece says "FIERO", and the small rear insert says "body by fisher" and has a picture of the Fisher coach. Not really sure who Fisher is, but all the GM cars from the 60's and 70's had little Fisher plaques on the rocker panel treads. I sold a set of Fisher tabs on Ebay for like $13. I usually pocket them at the junk yard (Shhhhh!!). I'm not sure when they stopped using them, none of my 86's have them.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||11-01-2000||04:36 PM||||The 84s had the Body by Fisher sill cover.  The 84-85's had the Fiero logo on the .5X5 sill cover.  The grey interiors had grey logos, the brown interiors had brown logos.  GM wanted to save $0.02 so they just made them black.  The sill covers pop out using a flat bladed screw driver. Try not to break the locking tab.  As you make your salvage yard runs, you can find them in many Fieros parked there.<BR>AS to value, it what ever you want to pay.  The brown ones seem to be the harder ones to find. I think I paid $10 at the FOCOA Nashville show in 97 for some brown ones.  But while I was at a Fiero bone yard this year I found a dozen of them in both colors.<P>Fisher was the coach designer for GM in the early days.. hence the carriage on the logo.||||reg||4
Z||000004||william_thorniii||11-01-2000||04:38 PM||||Rockcrawl,<P>Fisher was an actual coachmaker.  When GM purchased them to aid in the building of their "motorized coaches".  Fisher essentially became GM's frame shop.  If I remember my facts from my automotive history class from a few years ago. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000005||Fieroboy1||11-01-2000||06:24 PM||||The Fiero plant was the former fisher body plant.  It was renovated and used to make all fieros.  This is according to the Witzenburg book "Fiero".||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroparts.com||11-01-2000||06:45 PM||||??? Did you know that ALL Fieros have the word Fiero on that plate???(the long one)<P>Take a look at one (off the car) hold it under a bright light and you will see the word Fiero, it's hard to see on some.<P>later<P><P>------------------<BR>Tim||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||11-02-2000||04:14 AM||||My '84 has the "body by Fisher" plate, my '86 doesn't.  My brothers '86 Olds 88 has one of those plates on it, tho...<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-02-2000||08:37 PM||||My 87SC only has black in there. I don't care about the fisher tag but the Fiero labled one looks nice so I grabbed a set myself when I saw them. (I didn't like them in brown....)<P>Suprised me that they are 2 step molded and not hot printed. The 2 step modling is expensive. I'm not suprised GM stopped making them.<P>When you pry them out be careful. They are kind of brittle. I broke a couple of them first time I tried getting them out. <P>The fisher ones have a preference how they come out or go in. One end is made to go in first and come out last. Pry them out from the "body" end not the "coach" end. Don't pry in the long sides of them.||||reg||1
